Main Window
	
	XFaceMaker's main command window gives you access to the widgets and editing 
	functions you'll need to create your interface. It has three main
	sections: editing, toolkit, message. 
	
	EDITING SECTION
	
	The editing section includes the following features:
	
	-- The menu bar gives you access to most of the commands and editing
	windows in XFaceMaker. To give a command from a menu, open it and select an
	item from the pulldown menu. Many commands can also be given from the
	keyboard. 
	
	-- The Try mode checkbutton. Leave this unset when you are building or 
	editing the interface.  Change to Try mode when you want to test some
	aspect of the interface, to see how it will work in your final
	application.
	
	-- The editing icons on the top left of the window give you quick access 
	to the edit and display
	features of XFaceMaker. For more details, see the Help on Icons.
	
	-- An area to display the interface as a list of widgets or as a 
	widget tree. You can use the Widget List and Widget Tree to select and edit widgets. You
	can display one or both by clicking on the corresponding icons. You can
	adjust the space between the List and Tree by dragging the arrow found
	between the two.
	
	-- The "Current File" field displays the path name of the current interface
	file.
	
	-- The "Current Widget" field displays the name of the most recently
	selected widget.
	
	
	TOOLKIT SECTION
	
	The toolkit gives you access to all the widgets available for building
	your interface. Widgets are grouped by class in "widgetstores": Shells, 
	Composites, Primitives, Menus, Dialog, NSL, and Global. In addition, 
	any templates, groups, or classes you've added to your interface,
	can be accessed in the Template, Group, and User-defined widgetstores.
	
	Open a widgetstore by selecting its name in the "Toolkit" pulldown 
	menu. The widget icons are displayed in the display field. When the 
	pointer is over an icon, the widget's type is displayed. 
	
	You can open more than one widgetstore at a time, by deselecting the
	"Single" checkbutton, and selecting the widgetstores in the "Toolkit"
	pulldown menu.
	
	
	MESSAGE SECTION
	
	The message section of the main window has the following features:
	
	-- The status indicator at the top of the section displays the current
	internal condition of XFaceMaker. For instance, after startup, it states "XFaceMaker 
	Ready". When saving files, the Status indicator shows whether the 
	operation has succeeded or failed.
	
	-- The message window lists errors that occur while interpreting FACE
	scripts or creating widgets. These messages may be internal to XFaceMaker,
	may be produced by the Motif library or can be X Toolkit Intrinsics 
	library warnings. Clicking on the "Clear" button clears the message window.
	
	-- A set of icons to the left of the message window indicate the following.
	When the safe at the top is closed, the state of the Design process
	has been saved: when open, the state has not been saved. When the Design
	palette and the XFM Process icons (below the safe) are
	linked, the two processes are connected. 
	
Resource Window
	This window edits the resources of a widget.
	The top of the window identifies the widget by
	displaying path of the widget's parent, the name of
	the widget, its class, and any templates used to
	build the widget.  You may change any of these fields
	except widget's parent.
	
	The `Current Selection' toggle indicates whether
	or not this window will reflect the currently
	selected widget.  If set, whenever you change
	the object currently being edited, the window
	updates its contents to reflect the current
	widget.  If not set, then the widget updates
	its contents only when a change occurs to the
	widget specified in the top portion of the window.
	While you may have several resource windows open
	at the same time, only one may have this toggle
	button set.
	
	The `x' and `y' text fields indicate the position
	of the top left corner of the widget.  If the
	`auto pos' toggle is set, then these values become
	plain text fields which are not editable, since
	the widget determines its position by itself,
	perhaps according to its other resources.  Similarly,
	the `width' and `height' fields indicate the size of
	the widget.  If the `auto size' toggle is set, then
	these values become plain text fields, and the widget
	determines its own size
	
	The `popup' toggle indicates whether or not a shell
	widget is a popup widget.  It is insensitive for
	non-shell widgets.
	
	The `gadget' toggle indicates whether or not a widget
	is created as a gadget.  It is insensitive for widgets
	that do not have a gadget counterpart.
	
	To the left is a graphic of a `real'
	window, showing symbols of interface objects behind it.
	When the window icon appears opened, then the resource
	window has been edited, and its contents do not
	necessarily reflect the actual values for its
	corresponding widget.  If the window appears closed,
	then the resource window is up to date.
	
	Below this basic information is the actual list of
	resources for the widgets.  It comes in two forms:
	a list of resources, or a resource text editor.
	The list of resources allows you to see and edit
	the individual resource values, to control from
	where there values come, and to invoke specialized
	dialog boxes for manipulating the various resource
	types.  The text editor allows you to edit the
	widget's resources using a text editor.  You can switch
	between the two forms using the `Edit/List' button.
	See the `On List' and `On Editor' buttons of the `Help'
	menu in the resource window for details.
	
	Beneath either the resource list or resource editor
	are the following buttons:
	
	- Close: close this resource window;
	
	- Rebuild: apply the resources set for this widget
	by destroying and recreating the widget; in some
	cases (e.g. XmForms) this may also rebuild ancestors
	of the widget;
	
	- Set Values: apply the resources set for this widget
	by calling XtSetValues; this is faster than a Rebuild
	but sometimes yields different results, depending on
	how the widget is implemented; use `Set Values'
	to quickly apply repeated resource changes, then use
	`Rebuild' to check the correct state of the widget;
	
	- ... : pop a dialog box with which you can choose
	how and to which widgets the resources will be applied;
	see the Help button of the box for details;
	
	- Edit/List: switch between the resource list and the
	textual resource editor;
	
	- Sort: display the sort options sub-window; see
	Help/On List Sort for details;
	
	- Help: display help boxes.

MenuEditor
	Use this window to build and edit menus.  This
	window only presents the most commonly used
	resources.  You may still use the Resources
	window to edit other resources for menu items
	selected in this window.
	
	To start, use the Toolkit window to create an
	empty menu bar, popup menu, or option menu.
	Then, select the menu you just created.  You may
	then create buttons, labels, separators, and
	cascade menus in the menu.  Fill in the dialog
	portion below the line with the information for
	the menu item.  Be sure to set the item type.
	Then press the Add button to add the menu item.
	Repeat for more menu items.  After pressing Add,
	the values in the dialog box remain so that they
	may be used for subsequent menu items.
	
	The Replace button sets the resources for the
	currently selected menu item to those in the
	dialog box.  The Delete button deletes the
	currently selected menu item.
	
	The menu containing the currently selected menu
	item appears at the top of the box above the
	separator line.  If the item is in a cascade
	menu, the cascades leading up to the menu will be
	displayed.  The cascade button for each sub-menu
	is highlighted, as well as the currently selected
	menu item.  Increase the size of this window if
	not all of the menus are visible.
	
	While this window displays menu items in the
	correct font, it does not show any additional
	menu item decorations, except mnemonics.
	Specifically, toggle indicators, cascade
	indicators, and accelerators will not be shown in
	this window.  You must select the menu item and
	view the dialog box to see these resources.
	
	You may select a menu item by clicking on it in
	the menu above the line.  You may open a cascade
	menu by double-clicking its cascade button.  You
	may change the order of menu items by dragging
	them in their menu displayed above the line.
	
	There are some other miscellaneous buttons in
	this window:  The GLS button in the Label field
	inserts GLS prefixes in the label and mnemonic
	(if present).  The Select button in the Font
	field opens a font selection box to select a font
	name.  The arrow button in the Callbacks field
	rotates among the callbacks for that menu item.
	The "..." button in the Callbacks field opens an
	edit window to edit a multi-line callback
	script.

Debugger
	
	This window gives accesss to the debugging facilities
	of the FACE language. With the FACE debugger, it is
	possible to:
	
		- trace execution of FACE scripts,
	
		- execute instructions step-by-step,
	
		- print the stack of script and function calls,
		
		- set named breakpoints,
		
		- interrupt and abort execution of scripts,
		
		- execute FACE statements to print variables, etc.
	
	 The top part of the window contains information fields:
	a message field where the current status of the debugger
	is printed, a field containing the name of the script
	being executed and a field showing the name of the widget
	for which a script is executed.
	
	 Below is a vertical paned window containing three parts:
	the top part is where the source of the current script is
	shown, the middle part is where the debugger prints its
	output, and the bottom part is where you can type FACE
	statements to be executed during debugging. You can adjust
	the relative sizes of the three parts. Note that the first
	two text areas are read-only: in particular, if you want
	to modify a script, you have to switch back to edit mode if
	necessary and edit the script in the usual XFaceMaker editing
	window.
	
	 The three toggle buttons below the text areas are used to
	control the execution mode of the debugger.
	
	 The `Debug On' toggle activates or de-activates the FACE
	debugger: if this toggle is not set, no FACE debugging
	actions will take place. (This buttons is automatically
	unset when the FACE debugger window is hidden). If you do
	not use debugging, you should unset `Debug On' since
	debugging slows down the execution of scripts.
	
	 The `Step by step'
	button controls wether the FACE debugger stops before it
	executes each instruction or not. In step-by-step mode,
	you can only use the FACE debugger window and control the
	execution with the `Step', `Abort' and 	`Continue' buttons:
	the rest of the XFaceMaker interface as well as your user
	interface is locked.
	
	 When the  `Trace' toggle is set,
	FACE debugger traces the execution of all instructions by
	showing the source of the corresponding script, and by
	highlighting the current instruction. When this button
	is not set, the debugger only traces errors, warnings and
	breakpoints by showing the corresponding source and
	instruction, and by entering step-by-step mode.
	
	 On the right of the toggles are three push buttons.
	
	 The `Print Stack' button prints the stack of scripts
	and functions currently called, with the argument types
	and values, in the output text area.
	
	 The `Execute' button executes the FACE statements that
	you have typed in the xecution text area just above. If
	you are in step-by-step mode, the FACE statements are executed
	as if they were part of the currently debugged script: the
	local variables of the debugged scripts are accessible as well
	as all the global variables defined so far (you do not need to
	use the `global' keyword before referencing global variables in
	this case).
	
	 The `Clear' button clears the debugger output area.
	 
	 The bottom part of the window contains a row of push buttons:
	
		- `Close' hides the debugger window (and unsets `Tracing On');
		
		- `Step' advances to the next instruction in step-by-step
		   mode;
		
		- `Interrupt' is used to stop the execution of a script:
		   a SIGINT signal is sent to the design process, and the
		   debugger switches to step-by-step mode;
		
		- `Abort' is used to terminate execution of the current script
		   in step-by-step mode;
		
		- `Continue' is used to switch to normal run mode when in
		  step-by-step mode: execution continues without stopping
		  at each instruction;
		
		- `Help' shows this help message.
	
	 You can set breakpoints in scripts by inserting calls to the `breakpoint'
	function, which takes two arguments: a widget (usually `self') and the breakpoint
	name (a character string).
	When the breakpoint is reached, the debugger enters step-by-step mode and
	a message containing the breakpoint name is printed in the status area.
	
	 Errors and warnings encountered by the FACE parser or interpreter
	while debugging is on cause the debugger to stop at the point
	of the error and to enter step-by-step mode. Stepping, aborting
	or continuing after an execution error terminates the design process.
	The explanatory text of the error is printed in the debugger's
	output area as well as in the XFaceMaker message window.

Environment
	
	You can use this window to modify the environment variables of
	the XFaceMaker processes, for example to add a directory to
	the bitmaps path XBMLANGPATH.
	
	 You must first choose the environment variable you want to modify
	using the option menu at the top. The menu contains a number of
	pre-defined variables. You can select another variable using the
	`Other...' entry and then typing the variable name in the text field
	on the right.
	
	 If you selected a variable with a name ending with `PATH', a list
	appears with each entry containing an element of the path. You can
	add a path entry by typing a value in the bottom text field and clicking
	in the `Add' button (the new entry is inserted after the selected entry
	in the list, or at the end if no entry is selected). The `Delete' button
	removes the selected entry from the list.
	
	 If the variable is not a path, only the bottom text field appears.
	
	 The `Select...' button pops the file selector, thus allowing you to
	choose a directory name which will be put in the text field.
	
	 The `Apply' button sets the selected environment variable to the
	currently displayed value. For a path variable, the items in the
	list are concatenated and separated with `:'. The environment variable
	is changed using the `putenv' function in both the XFaceMaker process
	and the design process. Environment variables changed using this window
	are saved in the `.xfm.startup' file when the `Save Prefs' command is
	called.
	
	 The `OK' button does an `Apply' and unmaps the window.
	
	 The `Close' button only unmaps the window, leaving the environment
	unchanged (since the last `Apply').
	
	 `Help' displays this text.

Make Output
	
	When the `Make Appli' command is used, the output from
	the command launched to build your application is redirected
	to this window. The default build command is `make', but you can
	customize the build process by providing an executable file
	called `xfmmkappli' either in the current directory, in your home
	directory or in the system-wide directory containing XFaceMaker
	data files (usually `/usr/lib/X11/xfm').
	
	 If your application fails to build correctly, make sure you have
	generated the application files using the `Save' or `Save As' command
	with both the `Save C Code Also' and `Save Appli Files Also' options.
	Application files include the C code of the interface (Name.c),
	the main C file of the application (NameMain.c), the include file
	containing the function and active values declarations (NameApp.h),
	the C file containing the skeletons of the functions called from
	your FACE scripts (NameApp.c), and the Makefile used to build the
	whole application (Makefile).
	
	 You can edit NameMain.c, NameApp.c and Makefile using the commands
	`Edit Main', `Edit Functions' and `Edit Makefile'. XFaceMaker will
	launch the editor designated by the FMEDITOR environment variable
	(yb default `xedit'): this can be changed using the Environment
	window.
	
	 You can close the window using the `Close' button (but the window
	will pop up again every time new input will be read from the build
	command), and clear the existing output with the `Clear' button.
	
Active Values
	
	The Active Values box is used to create and modify the active
	values associated to an object.
	
	Active values are a very important concept in XFaceMaker. They are
	used to exchange data between the user interface and the application
	code, but also to define widget class resources, C++ class members
	and methods, and Drag&Drop side-effects. Each of these usages
	corresponds to a toggle button in the Active Values box. When
	a particular toggle is set, the Active Values boxe's appearance
	adapts to the particular needs of the selected usage.
	
	To edit active values for communicating between the interface and the application,
	see `Interface active values'.
	
	To edit active values for defining new resources in a widget class,
	see `Class active values'.
	
	To edit active values for defining members and methods in a C++ class,
	see `C++ active values'.
	
	To edit active values for defining drag source targets,
	see `Drag active values'.
	
	To edit active values for defining drop site targets,
	see `Drop active values'.
	
Interface active values
	
	An active value whose usage is set as "interface" is used to share data 
	with the application, or to define a script, a pseudo-resource, or a method. 
	
	The box has the following fields:
	
	"Usage"  Specify the usage for the active value.
		
	"Active Value Name" Enter a name to identify the active value. 
	The name must begin with an alphabetic character.
	
	"Type" Enter a type of variable for the active value. If no type is 
	specified, the type is "Any". The ellipsis pops up a type selection box.
	
	"Value" If storage is per widget and allocated automatically by XFaceMaker, 
	you may enter an initial value for the active value here.
	
	"Storage" Specify data storage and how it is allocated. There are three choices:
		
	-- "global": if the entire interface may always use the same data.
		
	-- "per widget": if you want separate storage locations for each widget in the interface
	that uses the active value.
		
	-- "per call": use this if you want to define interface set scripts that can be
	called with arguments. XFaceMaker will allocate storage each time it 
	calls the active value's set script---and then de-allocate it after the 
	script is executed.
	
	"Allocate automatically" When this is set, XFaceMaker will allocate storage for
	the active value if storage has not already been allocated. 
	
	"Access" You must decide whether your FACE script will refer to the contents 
	of the active value via the @ special variable, or by its immediate 
	value using $. If by immediate value, select the checkbutton marked 
	"immediate".
	"A list of active values"  Only active values that have the same usage appear in 
	this field, if you are using "new style" active values (version 3.0 
	and later). However, older .fm files with "old style" active values will 
	display all the active values of the widget, regardless of their usage.
		
	"Custom Editor"  Opens the editor you specified using the FMEDITOR environment variable. 
	
	"Clear" Clears the editing field.
	
	"Get/Set Script"  Enter get and set scripts for the active value. By 
	convention, the get script updates the active value's data to reflect the 
	interface's current setting. The set script tells the interface that the 
	data has changed so that the interface can change its presentation.
	
	"Add" Defines the active value for the current widget and registers the changes.
	
	"Replace"  Replaces the active value selected  in the display box with the newly
	defined active value.
	
	"Delete" Deletes the selected active value and its get and set scripts.
	
	The "up" and "down" arrows move the selected active value one position
	up or down in the active values list.
	
	"Close"  Closes the  window. If you have added (by clicking 
	on the Add button) an active value to the widget you are editing, the 
	active value is applied to the widget. If not, clicking Close merely closes 
	the window without changing the widget.
Class active values
	
	An active value whose usage is set to "widget class" defines a resource for 
	a widget class. To edit an active value of this kind, you must first select 
	the top level widget of the class. Specify the class name and class icon--
	they are associated to the widget. Then define as many resources as you 
	like for this class, and do one overall "Save". (You can also save your class 
	using the commands in the Classes sub-menu of the Modules menu.)
	
	This box has the following fields:
	
	"Usage"  Specify the usage for the active value.
	
	"Class Name" Specify the name of the class being defined or edited. 
	The ellipsis opens a list of already-defined classes from which to choose. 
	
	"New Resource Name" Enter the resource name when you are defining a 
	new resource, editing an existing resource, or deleting a resource.
	
	"Edit" This button pops up the Class Name box from which you may select the 
	class name that you want to edit.
	
	"Class Icon" Enter the name of a pixmap to represent the class in the 
	widgetstore. The ellipsis pops up the Pixmap editor for defining a pixmap.
	
	"Type" Used to specify the active value's type. in the "Type" field, or click 
	the ellipsis to pop up the Type Selector box. Choose a type from the list 
	or enter one in the "Resource type" text field, then click OK. 
	
	"Value" Enter a default value for the new resource being defined in the 
	"Value" field. Or click on the ellipsis to open an editing window for that 
	type of resource. Enter a value, then click on the Add button. Or click 
	on the List button to return to the Active Values box without specifying
	a value.
	
	"Methods..." Pops up a list of methods available for your widget class. You 
	can select a method name from the list or enter a name directly in the 
	text field of the Method Selector box. Then click the OK button.
	"A list of active values"  Only active values that have the same usage appear in 
	this field, if you are using "new style" active values (version 3.0 
	and later). However, older .fm files with "old style" active values will 
	display all the active values of the widget, regardless of their usage.
		
	"Custom Editor"  Opens the editor you specified using the FMEDITOR environment variable. 
	
	"Clear" Clears the editing field.
	
	"Get/Set Script"  Enter get and set scripts for the active value. By 
	convention, the get script updates the active value's data to reflect the 
	interface's current setting. The set script tells the interface that the 
